#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "f0520e86-8df9-5090-8d5c-761a4ba20583" :question/math-academy-id "e-6565" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n- $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n\n- $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n\n- $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0\n$$\n\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x\n$$\n\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0\n$$\n\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f,$ we have\n\n$$\ny'' + x^3 y = 8.\n$$\n\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "45688683-2788-58bd-8cf5-80feab863551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6566" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n\n- $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n\n- $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny''+y'=x \\cos x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0,$ and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nyy'' - y'+ 9 = 0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "1826d315-cef0-5b5f-b654-61733a63bf2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6567" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n\n- $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1,  \\quad  y'(4)= 1$\n\n- $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0,  \\quad  y'(1)= 5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4.$ So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151888 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "4f4f4539-466a-5cc0-9470-8b94d6221093" :question/math-academy-id "e-6568" :question/problem "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341351 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23ab976c-a6c8-514a-b253-c04885273eb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341353} {:answer/id #uuid "8d16ec67-6a8f-5dd9-b23a-de08f2f57d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341354} {:answer/id #uuid "f91f34e3-2389-5e61-849e-d29577efef65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341355} {:answer/id #uuid "72f1d168-88f9-50fc-ab42-d347f60ce5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341356} {:answer/id #uuid "2c12d868-abdc-5533-a8ee-f96e4d1e0359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341357}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f91f34e3-2389-5e61-849e-d29577efef65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341355} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8ba8465-316c-534e-95d1-d3398ee9789c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e02d60f-4fa6-583c-af1a-2b15f3ba4c02" :question/math-academy-id "q-130671" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=2$\n- ${y}^{3}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-xy=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+xy{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=2\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,b(x)=-x,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)=2.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-xy=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${y}^{3}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+xy{y}^{′}+{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $xy{y}^{′}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d864a1be-7032-5c3a-b185-ee874f9a65b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341360} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c69b78-4c10-56a9-a669-f96e1a554d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341361} {:answer/id #uuid "3e81ce27-b077-5b33-8b4d-e53f5d7bc5ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341362} {:answer/id #uuid "ca57bdf7-1a3c-57f2-85f2-723c4a9b864a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341363} {:answer/id #uuid "f3a746e8-52d1-5a04-bba5-327b860e9106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d864a1be-7032-5c3a-b185-ee874f9a65b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341360} :answer-field/id #uuid "d52c0b07-90f8-5624-8c46-8cc2e627366a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da59e353-90f8-5631-b467-4aff835f19bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-130675" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- ${e}^{2y}{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+3=0$\n- ${e}^{x}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2$\n- ${y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+5y=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{e}^{2y}{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+3=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term ${e}^{2y}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=2\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)={e}^{x},b(x)=4,c(x)=0,$ and $f(x)=2+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-2x{y}^{′}+5y=x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=-2x,c(x)=5,$ and $f(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d1873d6-cb10-583f-b48d-fa4e9dd2110b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341367} {:answer/id #uuid "01a7edac-65b4-55b2-b841-321337998ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341368} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d1e1af-11f4-59bb-8e93-18b2c0721be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+3y=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341369} {:answer/id #uuid "580c7842-e8a1-5375-b0cb-682d9253df09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341370} {:answer/id #uuid "953b859e-e79c-5a28-a1ab-b3d691bb31d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+3y=\\frac{x}{x-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341371}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d1873d6-cb10-583f-b48d-fa4e9dd2110b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+3y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341367} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd5e249f-97c8-5674-b08e-67fec4f0ba87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab9d13a9-1976-536d-bfec-9829d43ce57a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130994" :question/problem "For the differential equation $(x-2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+3y=x,$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n(x-2){y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+3y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f707b7b8-ab16-55a2-8396-6c0d6a5362d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}-y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341374} {:answer/id #uuid "74f85049-fd80-5f31-8912-242efdd76111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341375} {:answer/id #uuid "2cbdae09-107a-5ccc-9c79-1d465c9a5378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341376} {:answer/id #uuid "d5594ba2-072f-5ae5-bd34-3bc5718aada8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341377} {:answer/id #uuid "1184f437-b848-5bf7-a89a-2ac02dd56283" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{x+1}{x}{y}^{′}-y={e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f707b7b8-ab16-55a2-8396-6c0d6a5362d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}-y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341374} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf05e959-ea8c-5cf6-bfd2-0d4d678cf741" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69f670aa-e26f-55f5-ae96-ac7b02fbdf63" :question/math-academy-id "q-130996" :question/problem "For the differential equation $x{y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}-y=x{e}^{x},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}-y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341379 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb98d297-b305-5b1f-aabb-31785113c708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341381} {:answer/id #uuid "7005190e-e172-58bd-986b-51724efb5d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341382} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3f2c93-6a05-50d5-bb43-35203c048ef0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341383} {:answer/id #uuid "628c02e9-e153-5d65-8232-75b1538b6bc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341384} {:answer/id #uuid "ec4604f3-de94-5e52-b7d6-ef5649b76ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec4604f3-de94-5e52-b7d6-ef5649b76ca7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341385} :answer-field/id #uuid "f97b2fa5-35c7-528d-8346-6234742f1220" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d885e48-2e79-58d6-9707-a6c9e1d542f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-131001" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+y=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=1,\\,y(\\pi )=1,\\,{y}^{′}(\\pi )=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=x{e}^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=\\pi $).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b3ade63-1e70-501f-ae43-08bc563a5b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149789} {:answer/id #uuid "cbef941c-4f70-50ba-a00f-108f81bc0da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbef941c-4f70-50ba-a00f-108f81bc0da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149790} :answer-field/id #uuid "e873100d-c852-5d87-9cbb-248e4cc13e84" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149788} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eebd4864-8ce9-546e-87ad-9e7a376b562a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149792} {:answer/id #uuid "ef368c32-84e1-5d1d-afa9-f181948d778a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149793}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eebd4864-8ce9-546e-87ad-9e7a376b562a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149792} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2ca1e7a-9f7c-55ce-99ca-b6bd65a1a751" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149791} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58bfac0e-9113-533e-8329-967ecc57984b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149795} {:answer/id #uuid "637e000c-17f3-59b1-8a76-00e5e3458d1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58bfac0e-9113-533e-8329-967ecc57984b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149795} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad919cb4-01ef-5ee6-8993-0a040c1460a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7830240-8585-5e21-ba7a-c44cace370a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-337919" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=2$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy-2x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+y=2\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=2,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy-2x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy=2x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=2x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3a1c028-7717-55db-b8a3-fbe577da5ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149798} {:answer/id #uuid "3558e504-c815-5bdb-904b-6b499c98a79e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3a1c028-7717-55db-b8a3-fbe577da5ac9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149798} :answer-field/id #uuid "600fbb74-bdbd-5116-97b9-b2a5cd79d9b0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149797} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5030bc4-4753-5b61-a957-f308fdf7daee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149801} {:answer/id #uuid "de118a19-6e65-5980-a6d7-0def901542e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5030bc4-4753-5b61-a957-f308fdf7daee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149801} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e245b17-4874-510f-a799-adb5a6f3f08b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149800} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df70e34f-2742-563a-b5a9-c191427a8257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149804} {:answer/id #uuid "5edabb40-bdd5-5070-97d3-bcda1993b06e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df70e34f-2742-563a-b5a9-c191427a8257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149804} :answer-field/id #uuid "81c68ad2-f693-5f7e-ba69-d5b504b3c6d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4e93ecbe-42b9-5aed-a2ce-1b2b418237f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-337990" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}-y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)={e}^{x},$ which is different from zero.\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y+x=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=-x.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-x,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0755616e-8d6f-50ce-82cd-8c3124d45f72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341388} {:answer/id #uuid "e16c05fe-b3a8-50d5-ab83-3f44716c3fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e16c05fe-b3a8-50d5-ab83-3f44716c3fc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341389} :answer-field/id #uuid "64dfac85-867c-5d93-abdd-a093526fff34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341387} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42384a7c-cd2c-5e84-97e8-1be0ef4192ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341391} {:answer/id #uuid "2e727099-ce63-54df-b3c8-6be4852bed67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42384a7c-cd2c-5e84-97e8-1be0ef4192ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341391} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a7bfec-c698-5162-9d1d-70b6585ae610" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341390} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e407a07-7097-5845-9355-c9ad8823438e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341394} {:answer/id #uuid "c916f550-13d3-5b42-9796-6f02c236fda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c916f550-13d3-5b42-9796-6f02c236fda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341395} :answer-field/id #uuid "fee27974-b815-5e20-b40c-bd897c398114" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fc00c12-63c8-559a-aa45-1b909350bd43" :question/math-academy-id "q-337996" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=7\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=7,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x){y}^{″}+(1-x){y}^{′}-4y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy+{x}^{3}=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+xy=-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-{x}^{3},$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341396 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da2ec0d-7545-5eef-bd82-4770331113cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341398} {:answer/id #uuid "1f45c620-91e3-5ed3-b3de-eb8a83dbdf6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0da2ec0d-7545-5eef-bd82-4770331113cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341398} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a0d79aa-fe53-5b28-9a0a-b22988e07e52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341397} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3e93038-f923-5254-8c9c-b06ec0b21d0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341401} {:answer/id #uuid "4da11eb8-a953-5e26-b6de-d746d7ecc41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4da11eb8-a953-5e26-b6de-d746d7ecc41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341402} :answer-field/id #uuid "6be2fc62-3d43-58ab-bbde-3aa171f03137" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341400} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2544705d-d7eb-5b31-9f45-ac88c47750cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341404} {:answer/id #uuid "0a3f4971-d719-5477-b57c-2b84cd9433a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2544705d-d7eb-5b31-9f45-ac88c47750cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341404} :answer-field/id #uuid "5578a46d-66a2-5b12-ac67-d690c03055b0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9bd10097-6fe9-5533-b23b-68beefc52771" :question/math-academy-id "q-338006" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+xy=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=4x$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+xy=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-2y=4x\n$$\n\nis $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=4x,$ which is different from zero.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{x}^{2}y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6945ac5-5b14-50da-ba3a-e02a47e25203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341408} {:answer/id #uuid "ace65b49-4419-5718-8126-2e2057f2ea39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6945ac5-5b14-50da-ba3a-e02a47e25203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341408} :answer-field/id #uuid "67d1ac7f-49ac-53f3-b2b5-97f2c1011a54" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341407} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6422240b-f86a-5471-9ed1-f86e721a8810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341411} {:answer/id #uuid "694551d3-1a8a-5eb8-8650-df6f36571d69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341412}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6422240b-f86a-5471-9ed1-f86e721a8810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341411} :answer-field/id #uuid "98980f04-b0e7-5aa8-9878-0137594db6fd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341410} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119ad110-b97d-506f-b2c5-faffbc0a0477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341414} {:answer/id #uuid "908f158f-7201-5387-a9e3-fdc01404665a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "908f158f-7201-5387-a9e3-fdc01404665a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341415} :answer-field/id #uuid "249e9ea1-b271-5661-a0ca-c5a0da6cef5b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "916dc27d-5ed7-5589-9d6c-93214bdd310b" :question/math-academy-id "q-338087" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.$\\,$ The equation $\\,\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+y=0$ is {{field-1}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+3y=0$ is {{field-2}}. \n\n$\\,$ The equation $\\,{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y+9=0$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$ If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+(x-2){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+3y=0\n$$\n\nis $\\text{homogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=0$ for all $x.$\n\n- Writing the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y+9=0\n$$\n\nin the form $a{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+cy=f,$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,y=-9.\n$$\n\nThis equation is $\\text{inhomogeneous}$ since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x)=-9,$ which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47bad2b8-8d3e-57d6-82b3-2c3439f672a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341418} {:answer/id #uuid "35ff67c9-ece9-59f1-8612-b8e2b3c84de4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341419} {:answer/id #uuid "6da61333-a591-52aa-9ef7-07f3e1c29e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341420} {:answer/id #uuid "7558911d-9966-59ef-82bc-a27c1567a02c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341421} {:answer/id #uuid "7aca29ef-7d33-5dd9-8aa3-21f2f8e57080" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da61333-a591-52aa-9ef7-07f3e1c29e8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341420} :answer-field/id #uuid "6626197e-a518-52e5-8075-49de48d89a58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63a0e75a-e8ed-5555-a126-d694412fb15f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338102" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}$\n- $y{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x$\n- $5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+y={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=x,b(x)=0,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation \n\n$$\n5{y}^{″}-{x}^{2}{y}^{′}+7{y}^{2}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $7{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "549a6203-d92c-550d-a36f-ac104a959412" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341425} {:answer/id #uuid "4235cb7f-d7df-51e9-8750-f84e61256173" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341426} {:answer/id #uuid "910c7651-00de-59a3-8211-956e290418f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341427} {:answer/id #uuid "42e753f1-9f0b-54c5-af3b-00b78018e7c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341428} {:answer/id #uuid "730c36c7-13a2-5cad-a295-55c0003c22f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "730c36c7-13a2-5cad-a295-55c0003c22f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341429} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8bb9631-31f8-57b4-8dbf-afa49adc64d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79dba891-0fd5-5b33-acae-cf1ca4fcb237" :question/math-academy-id "q-338531" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+y={e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,y(2)=0$\n- ${y}^{″}+4y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1$\n- ${y}^{″}-y={x}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40c24394-e973-59d1-9167-634cf777f21e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341432} {:answer/id #uuid "8802af8b-4dde-5a58-a51e-084a51ffb842" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341433} {:answer/id #uuid "2abac178-ade9-5f83-9fe1-6252892ec145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341434} {:answer/id #uuid "13231aec-e1bb-5d83-b2ea-584488714b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341435} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d74180-5d78-5e19-a40e-3203ce946229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13231aec-e1bb-5d83-b2ea-584488714b61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341435} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8a9729b-f977-5b73-a7a7-48f5c43c8f50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341431}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec6d918c-1e89-56ba-9337-5b4cab88266e" :question/math-academy-id "q-338533" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=2,\\,y(5)=2$\n- ${y}^{″}+y={x}^{3}$\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(-3)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-3)=1$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=5$). This is a **boundary value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=-3$)."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188e19b8-04d9-5024-a1a0-560681b52a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341439} {:answer/id #uuid "3557fe35-3d8a-5b9f-8319-034a0b55e292" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341440} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ec3ff9-a194-5b32-b7b0-91f6b59a11e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341441} {:answer/id #uuid "5c65fb1c-74b3-5c07-a63e-9a134cc05155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341442} {:answer/id #uuid "7c29f87b-9169-55a0-9fcd-db3d482e3a30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1ec3ff9-a194-5b32-b7b0-91f6b59a11e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341441} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cf2381-7b48-5325-b87b-78fc4e994f54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7b3292e-ae09-5cdf-bbb6-9d5f24a711b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-339256" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+2y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=5x$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(4)=2$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$ and $x=4$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152550 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e063b066-9920-5bb2-be04-d33fbff25a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150211} {:answer/id #uuid "e6f3b9fe-91f9-5a98-bdd6-724fb05eef3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + y^{′} + y = x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150212} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d36733-3b48-5fb4-bf3a-5c6832523bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + y^{′} + 1 = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150213} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee98db9-499a-5555-8e43-26d5dfac9e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} + y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150214} {:answer/id #uuid "6a33f465-fdf8-5b19-8d26-527a8dc16a23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} + \\frac{y^{′}}{x} + \\frac{y}{x^{2}} = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ee98db9-499a-5555-8e43-26d5dfac9e71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} + y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150214} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bceb33-adf8-542f-8ea2-c0463df59142" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "392fa1c6-b529-58c2-a651-f4ff011c9161" :question/math-academy-id "q-64963" :question/problem "For the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y={x}^{3},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3f89cc8-a50b-5de8-9c3d-27ec8e2d4143" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150229} {:answer/id #uuid "e1aec038-9f0a-5951-adda-27a24ac74322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150230} {:answer/id #uuid "6401077c-8c5d-5249-8c28-c40dcf280ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150231} {:answer/id #uuid "64633c6f-fa31-5827-b308-56446f88686b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150232} {:answer/id #uuid "68437b04-cdad-5926-9c70-7e228b76fc22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6401077c-8c5d-5249-8c28-c40dcf280ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150231} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e472346-b9a3-5a4e-8320-ab47a8c49103" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150228}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c0602e3-c50d-5991-b1fc-f704c082ddef" :question/math-academy-id "q-65014" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n- $x{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+y={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=10$\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=0$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y,$ then this is an **initial value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\n- The second system is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is not an initial value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=2$). This is a **boundary value problem.**"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152554 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb04a28-f31d-5fe8-9f43-1594cdd9b87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} - y^{′} + 3y = 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150235} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f1dee2-5d44-5e3b-ba3c-5b3dd179197e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - y^{′} + 3y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150236} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7b9b1-1f9d-5306-8c37-06b3080dfd73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} - y^{′} + 3y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150237} {:answer/id #uuid "11787f33-537d-5419-a8a3-8e16a20510be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{″} - \\frac{y^{′}}{x^{2}} + \\frac{3y}{x^{2}} = \\frac{e^{x}}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150238} {:answer/id #uuid "500f5288-3a89-5a1f-ad8f-607a26219b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} - y^{′} = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7b9b1-1f9d-5306-8c37-06b3080dfd73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}y^{″} - y^{′} + 3y = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150237} :answer-field/id #uuid "111318d4-2f2c-5f8d-9be4-faeb5239eb2b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150234}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "131a8593-ee2e-5d10-85e8-bff07e98bf94" :question/math-academy-id "q-65017" :question/problem "For the differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+3y={e}^{x},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}+3y=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d7898511-4336-55dd-af09-f5f8afe6bf87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341446} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d1aeee-2739-5c4a-baf5-3b627075fc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341447} {:answer/id #uuid "71363472-5521-5444-b405-94901448a188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341448} {:answer/id #uuid "a55ec0a2-6bbb-5d2a-84f9-f7e48321295f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341449} {:answer/id #uuid "acee5be6-190f-5ed5-a9ae-b82b3fcdd16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8d1aeee-2739-5c4a-baf5-3b627075fc06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341447} :answer-field/id #uuid "c62fa06c-cdaa-5805-9e78-7d3c1e1345a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341445}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "166fcd62-ada1-5a0c-a703-fe872e3fc63c" :question/math-academy-id "q-93715" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- ${y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}+y=x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=-x,c(x)=1,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y{y}^{′}=0\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $y{y}^{′}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}+y=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $\\frac{1}{y}{y}^{″}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d4357e-5d94-57d3-bf28-de364df8aa32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341453} {:answer/id #uuid "796af2d8-3cb3-5bf0-9137-370ed0d9102b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341454} {:answer/id #uuid "945961fe-2c6d-529c-87e0-7ab2efe873bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341455} {:answer/id #uuid "7d866803-b1f6-5fe1-bbea-38c8c0b7bbcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341456} {:answer/id #uuid "e65b145a-16e6-5f2a-aaf7-4f776e4acff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d4357e-5d94-57d3-bf28-de364df8aa32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341453} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d4ced10-83a3-51c8-9db0-13b6654e303b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01fc92bc-36e8-54db-9a2e-368326538d04" :question/math-academy-id "q-93721" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n- $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x$\n- $\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- ${y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "А second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}-{e}^{x}{y}^{′}=x\n$$\n\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $2{y}^{2}{y}^{″}.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=\\frac{1}{x},b(x)=1,c(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ and $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+17y=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x)=1,b(x)=0,c(x)=17,$ and $f(x)=2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "baac910b-772d-5934-afdb-6bf36a05c9fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{6}{x-1}{y}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{(x-1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341460} {:answer/id #uuid "99a69dc3-b54c-533d-8221-a15e7983bb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{″}+\\frac{6}{x-1}{y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341461} {:answer/id #uuid "97bca613-204d-5a0c-93f2-f9e89b5996a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+6(x-1){y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341462} {:answer/id #uuid "b591dd7e-67bc-592a-9483-208f99afb88f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}=2{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341463} {:answer/id #uuid "ea44105b-4a82-59bf-9a98-c0da46d76a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97bca613-204d-5a0c-93f2-f9e89b5996a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+6(x-1){y}^{′}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341462} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a4b8892-e3f3-56ae-b719-37bd077420e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341459}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aeba1546-0b0b-5f1d-9ecb-30a8dfd77cc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-93781" :question/problem "For the differential equation $(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+6(x-1){y}^{′}=2{x}^{3},$ what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=f(x).\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x){y}^{″}+b(x){y}^{′}+c(x)y=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}{y}^{″}+6(x-1){y}^{′}=0.\n$$"}]}
